{"id":"b8bf0955304a","publishedAt":null,"posts":[[{"text":"有識者「アメリカ人の100割が嫌いな『日本料理』がこれ🤮」"},{"br":1},{"text":"2026年11月23日10:00:00"},{"br":1},{"href":"http://www.gurum.biz/archives/100365417.html#comments","text":"コメント(15)"},{"br":1},{"href":"http://www.gurum.biz/archives/category/%e3%82%ab%e3%83%ac%e3%83%bc%e3%83%bb%e3%82%b7%e3%83%81%e3%83%a5%e3%83%bc%e3%83%bb%e3%82%b9%e3%83%bc%e3%83%97","text":"カレー・シチュー・スープ"},{"br":1},{"href":"https://twitter.com/share","text":"Tweet"},{"br":1},{"text":"1 名前："},{"text":"名無し"},{"text":" 投稿日：2026/11/21 10:49:05 ID:7psPA3hYQ"},{"br":1},{"text":"I’m going to say a Japanese food that probably 100% of Americans will hate is a vegetable called \"goya.\" In English it’s called bitter melon or bitter gourd, but that really doesn’t mean much because since we don’t have it in America, even the word bitter melon is meaningless for us. \n \nIt’s a bumpy, green vegetable famous for its distinct bitter taste. And when I say bitter, damn, this stuff is really bitter. It is popular in Okinawan cuisine, but has spread throughout Japan, and you can find it in most regions. The most popular dish to use it is probably \"goya champuru.\" This dish mixes slices of goya with a firm tofu, thinly sliced pork belly, canned tuna, or American Spam, and beaten egg gently folded or scrambled into the mixture. It’s then topped with soy sauce, dashi, salt, and a generous topping of savory katsuobushi (dried bonito flakes). \n \nTo be honest, I don’t like it. In fact, I don’t like goya at all. It is bitter beyond my capacity to enjoy in any way. It is one of the few Japanese foods I won’t eat. I would wager that most Americans would feel the same. But goya is supposedly very nutritious, so if you are a visitor to Japan you can give yourself a challenge and decide for yourself."},{"br":1},{"text":"— Brent Swancer (@BrentSwancer) "},{"href":"https://x.com/BrentSwancer/status/2123719271498871019?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw","text":"November 20, 2026"},{"br":1},{"text":"2 名前："}